/.yarn/**            linguist-vendored
/.yarn/releases/*    binary
/.yarn/plugins/**/*  binary
/.pnp.*              binary linguist-generated

# Anything a shell executes has to stay LF, on every platform.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is the default on Windows, and it applies to checkout *and*
# to `git archive` on export. So a .sh committed from Windows reaches a macOS or
# Linux machine as CRLF and fails to parse -- at `case ... in`, or at
# `set -o pipefail`, with a message that points nowhere near the cause. It also
# silently breaks the husky hooks, which have no extension to key off.
#
# This is not hypothetical: the pre-push gate's own script was committed from
# Windows, arrived on the Mac with 170 stray CR bytes, and reported a syntax
# error 11 lines above the real problem.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
.husky/pre-*    text eol=lf
scripts/*.sh    text eol=lf
